WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:07.269
Usually what you see in a movie was put there
on purpose—but not always. We've already

00:00:07.269 --> 00:00:11.900
covered strange things accidentally included
in the background of movies, such as the unzipped

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:17.300
extra in Teen Wolf, the head-bonking Stormtrooper
in Star Wars, and a parked car in Braveheart

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.070
- all of which and more you can find in our
video, 20 Movie Mistakes You Never Noticed.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.320
But here are five more background bloopers
you can still catch on-screen...

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.679
Think fast

00:00:26.679 --> 00:00:29.529
There's an amazing moment in Being John Malkovich:

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.269
"Hey Malkovich, think fast!"

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.100
"Ah!"

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:37.230
While the scene calling for Malkovich to get
hit with the can seems to have been planned,

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:41.949
the "think fast" line apparently wasn't. Director
Spike Jonze said in an audio commentary that

00:00:41.949 --> 00:00:46.719
a bunch of extras working on the film snuck
a case of beer on set, and were getting "pretty

00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:50.530
lit." The director added that the line the
can-thrower yelled as the car zoomed past

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:56.649
earned him his Screen Actors Guild card, bumping
his pay from $100 a day to $700. Not a bad

00:00:56.649 --> 00:00:58.280
way to earn a raise.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.480
Hazard pay

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.750
During a key scene in The Last Samurai, Tom
Cruise's character arrives on horseback for

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:08.850
the film's epic final battle. As Cruise dismounts
from his horse, the animal can actually be

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:13.369
seen kicking one of the extras in the groin.
Instead of dropping to the ground and crying,

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:19.060
the extra simply takes a step back, reacts
silently, and regroups in formation. Samurai

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:22.430
are known for being disciplined. This guy
went the extra mile.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.159
A random cowboy

00:01:24.159 --> 00:01:28.369
Nobody watching Pirates of the Caribbean:
The Curse of the Black Pearl could've expected

00:01:28.369 --> 00:01:33.130
what went down in its final scene. After reclaiming
the Black Pearl, Captain Jack Sparrow orders

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:37.689
his merry band of pirates to get to work on
the deck. Just as they begin to move, a man

00:01:37.689 --> 00:01:42.470
wearing a white t-shirt and a cowboy hat can
be spotted in the background, looking off

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:48.270
into distant waters. Was this mysterious man's
cameo a secret Easter Egg, or perhaps a character

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:49.549
to be introduced in the sequels?

00:01:49.549 --> 00:01:54.710
No, he was supposedly just a grip crew member
who forgot to clear the set during filming.

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:55.820
Fraidy cat

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:59.530
Classic Bond villain Blofeld's white Persian
cat is a pretty creepy customer in You Only

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:04.180
Live Twice. The furry creature loses a bit
of luster when it legitimately freaks out

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.680
during one of the explosion scenes towards
the end of the film. Donald Pleasence, who

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.050
plays Blofeld, does his best to keep the cat
clutched tightly to his body; but for a few

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.710
brief moments, the camera captures the frantic
feline scrambling for the nearest possible

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:20.640
exit. It's safe to say the cat was both shaken
and stirred.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.350
Dog Days

00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:26.200
Remember the early '90s when pro-wrestler
Hulk Hogan attempted a movie career? It didn't

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.560
work. In fact, the only reason anyone talks
about his 1993 feature, Mister Nanny, has

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.870
nothing to do with the Hulkster. While Hogan
tools around on his motorcycle, the camera

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.700
captures one of the most bizarre sights in
cinema.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:41.700
Your eyes aren't playing tricks on you. Some
random dude threw a dog into the ocean.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:47.460
Why? Who is he? Did the dog stink or something?
Was the motorcycle-riding montage so important

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:50.100
that this footage needed to stay in the final
cut?

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:54.560
The only other question is what makes Hulk
Hogan prouder: riding his motorcycle in front

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.310
of a dog being thrown in the ocean, or that
time he opened a pasta restaurant in the Mall

00:02:58.310 --> 00:02:59.840
of America for some reason:

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.720
"Pastamania has got all my Hulkamaniacs running
wild. And I've eaten so many Hulkaroos and

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:08.710
Hulkayoos, I kinda feel sorry for Big Bubba
brother!"

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:13.120
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.070
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know which crazy background moments we

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:17.570
missed...

